// A reader that keeps 17 significant digits of the decimal text and then
// converts is wrong on this literal. The literal is the midpoint between `1.0`
// and its successor plus one digit, so it is strictly above the midpoint and
// the nearest double is the successor, `1.0000000000000002`
// (spec/01-lexical.md, the range of a Number literal). The text rounded to 17
// significant digits is `1.0000000000000001`, whose nearest double is `1.0`,
// and 17 digits is the obvious width to keep because that width round-trips
// every double and no shorter one does.
//
// Probe 251 already separates that reader, at its overflow row of 18
// significant digits. What no probe held is the same class in a fraction: a
// reader with 17 digits passes probes 243, 250 and 253, and fails only probe
// 251 and this one (issue #877).

print 1.000000000000000111022302462515654042363166809082031251;
print 1.000000000000000111022302462515654042363166809082031251 == 1;
print 1.000000000000000111022302462515654042363166809082031251 == 1.0000000000000002;

// STATUS: ok
// CHECK: 1.0000000000000002
// CHECK: false
// CHECK: true
